= ψ Quality Factor =

{{Plain_Hook
| in_one_sentence    = The ψ quality factor Q_ψ — defined as the ratio of stored ψ energy to power dissipated per radian — is the central engineering figure of merit for every [[ψ Resonator]]-based device, setting simultaneously the soliton lifetime, the drive power requirement, the frequency selectivity, and the Anchor burst duration; for the [[Psi Stabilizer]] it must exceed 200, and this requirement cascades directly into every composite material choice in the [[Smart Composite Fill]].
| everyday_analogy   = A wine glass struck with a fork rings for seconds — it has a high Q. The same note played into a room full of heavy curtains disappears almost immediately — low Q. A radio tuned to a weak station needs a high-Q antenna circuit to select that frequency precisely without pulling in neighbours. ψ quality factor captures both meanings simultaneously: it determines how long the resonator rings (soliton lifetime) and how narrowly it selects frequencies (mode purity). High Q is always preferable in ψ resonator design — but the composite material's loss tangent sets a ceiling that cannot be exceeded.
| what_it_predicts   = (1) A hard Q ceiling <math>Q_\psi^{\rm max} = 1/\tan\delta_\psi</math> from material loss, derivable from the [[ψ Loss Tangent]] (B4) composite budget; (2) A loaded Q below the ceiling after radiation and coupling losses are included, measurable directly by ringdown; (3) A module-specific Q target table showing that different applications require very different Q values — the [[Psi Defender]] deliberately uses low Q for broad-spectrum shielding while the [[ψ Memory Element]] requires Q > 10⁶ for hour-scale soliton retention; (4) A drive power scaling law showing that required drive power scales as <math>1/Q_\psi</math> — high-Q devices are more efficient to operate continuously.
| if_you_remember_one_thing = Q_ψ = 2π × (energy stored)/(energy lost per cycle). Doubling Q halves the required continuous drive power for the same stored ψ amplitude. But Q is bounded above by 1/tan δ_ψ — and that constraint is why every material choice in the [[Smart Composite Fill]] is driven by the loss budget in [[ψ Loss Tangent]] (B4).
}}

== Definition from the Stress-Energy Tensor ==

The ψ field energy stored in a volume <math>V</math> is derived from the time-time component of the ψ stress-energy tensor <math>T^{00}_\psi</math> (from [[Psionics]]):

:<math>U_\psi = \int_V T^{00}_\psi\,d^3x = \int_V \left[\tfrac{1}{2}(\partial_t\psi)^2 + \tfrac{1}{2}|\nabla\psi|^2 + \tfrac{1}{2}m_{\rm eff}^2\psi^2 + \tfrac{\lambda}{4}\psi^4\right]d^3x</math>

The power dissipated by the material:

:<math>P_{\rm diss} = \omega\,\varepsilon''_\psi\,\langle|\psi|^2\rangle\,V</math>

where <math>\varepsilon''_\psi</math> is the imaginary part of the complex permittivity (from [[ψ Loss Tangent]] B4) and <math>\langle|\psi|^2\rangle</math> is the volume-averaged squared amplitude.

The '''ψ quality factor''':

:<math>\boxed{Q_\psi = \frac{\omega\,U_\psi}{P_{\rm diss}}}</math>

{{Math_Bridge
| equation_label = ψ quality factor
| equation       = <math>Q_\psi = \frac{\omega\,U_\psi}{P_{\rm diss}}</math>
| in_english     =
* <math>Q_\psi</math> — dimensionless. Directly measures how many radians of oscillation occur before the stored energy falls to <math>1/e</math> of its initial value.
* <math>\omega</math> — angular frequency of the resonant mode. Higher frequency → same stored energy dissipated per unit time → Q harder to achieve at higher frequencies (partly why 40 kHz is chosen over microwave: material Q is higher at lower frequencies).
* <math>U_\psi</math> — total stored ψ field energy in the cavity. Depends on drive amplitude and cavity volume.
* <math>P_{\rm diss}</math> — power permanently removed from the ψ field each second. Sets the continuous drive power required to maintain constant amplitude.
* '''Engineering interpretation:''' The device must supply <math>P_{\rm drive} = P_{\rm diss} = \omega U_\psi / Q_\psi</math> of continuous ψ power to maintain a steady-state amplitude. High Q means lower required drive power at the same stored energy.
}}

=== Simplified Form for Linear Resonator ===

For a resonator with uniform mode field and loss-tangent-dominated dissipation:

:<math>Q_\psi = \frac{\varepsilon'_\psi}{\varepsilon''_\psi} = \frac{1}{\tan\delta_\psi}</math>

This is the material-limited Q. The full loaded Q is always less than this value.

== Loaded Q Decomposition ==

The total (loaded) quality factor combines three independent loss mechanisms:

:<math>\frac{1}{Q_L} = \frac{1}{Q_{\rm mat}} + \frac{1}{Q_{\rm rad}} + \frac{1}{Q_{\rm coup}}</math>

{{Math_Bridge
| equation_label = Loaded Q decomposition
| equation       = <math>\frac{1}{Q_L} = \frac{1}{Q_{\rm mat}} + \frac{1}{Q_{\rm rad}} + \frac{1}{Q_{\rm coup}}</math>
| in_english     =
* <math>Q_{\rm mat} = 1/\tan\delta_\psi</math> — material Q: losses from ψ absorption in the composite fill. Set by [[ψ Loss Tangent]] (B4). For the target composite: <math>Q_{\rm mat} = 1/0.005 = 200</math>.
* <math>Q_{\rm rad}</math> — radiation Q: ψ energy lost by leaking through the shell boundary. For a Neumann boundary (perfect reflector from [[ψ Boundary Conditions]] A5): <math>Q_{\rm rad} \to \infty</math>. For a Robin boundary (partial absorption): finite <math>Q_{\rm rad}</math> determined by the Robin coefficient <math>\alpha_R</math>. The [[Psi Stabilizer]] outer shell uses Robin conditions to allow controlled ψ leakage for OPM sensing — this reduces <math>Q_{\rm rad}</math>.
* <math>Q_{\rm coup}</math> — coupling Q: ψ energy extracted by the measurement/drive coupling system (the bifilar coil). For critical coupling: <math>Q_{\rm coup} = Q_{\rm mat}</math>, giving <math>Q_L = Q_{\rm mat}/2</math>.
* '''Design strategy:''' The [[Psi Stabilizer]] targets <math>Q_L > 200</math>. With <math>Q_{\rm mat} = 200</math>, this requires both <math>Q_{\rm rad}</math> and <math>Q_{\rm coup}</math> to be as high as possible. The Robin outer boundary coefficient must be minimised (less leakage), and the bifilar coil coupling must be under-coupled (<math>Q_{\rm coup} > Q_{\rm mat}</math>) to sacrifice some drive efficiency for higher loaded Q.
}}

