== Drive Power and Efficiency ==

At steady state, the drive must supply exactly the power dissipated:

:<math>P_{\rm drive} = P_{\rm diss} = \frac{\omega_0\,U_\psi}{Q_\psi}</math>

For the [[Psi Stabilizer]] at design point, with target stored energy <math>U_\psi</math> estimated from the ψ field amplitude needed to produce detectable HRV coherence shift (a TML-3 calibration quantity):

Assuming <math>U_\psi \sim 1</math> μJ (order-of-magnitude estimate pending TML-3):

:<math>P_{\rm drive} \approx \frac{2\pi \times 4\times10^4 \times 10^{-6}}{200} \approx 1.3\,\text{mW}</math>

This is well within the wearable power budget — a 500 mAh Li-ion cell at 3.7V provides approximately 6.6 kJ, giving continuous operation for <math>6600/0.0013 \approx 5\times10^6</math> seconds ≈ 58 days from material loss alone. The dominant power consumers are the electronics, not the ψ field drive. The [[BST Energy Recovery]] (D4) flyback recapture is therefore primarily relevant to the drive electronics efficiency, not the stored ψ field maintenance.

== Measurement: Ringdown Protocol ==

The most direct and reliable Q_ψ measurement is resonator ringdown:

{{Way_It_Works
| title       = Ringdown protocol for Q_ψ measurement
| step1_label = Drive to steady state
| step1_text  = Apply continuous drive to the resonator at the target resonant frequency <math>\omega_{010}</math> using the [[Bifilar Coil Engineering]] (D1) driver. Drive for a minimum of <math>10\tau</math> to ensure steady-state amplitude is reached. Monitor ψ-proxy signal (OPM output or bifilar coil back-EMF) until amplitude stabilises to within ±2%.
| step2_label = Cut drive
| step2_text  = Switch drive off in < 1 μs (GaN half-bridge gate switching time from [[HV Pulse Modulation]] D3). The fast cut prevents drive-signal contamination of the ringdown measurement.
| step3_label = Record decay
| step3_text  = Sample ψ-proxy signal at minimum 10× the resonant frequency (400 kHz sample rate for 40 kHz resonance). Record for at least <math>5\tau</math> to capture > 99% of the decay curve.
| step4_label = Fit exponential
| step4_text  = Fit the amplitude envelope of the recorded signal to <math>A(t) = A_0\,e^{-t/\tau}</math> using nonlinear least-squares (scipy.optimize.curve_fit or equivalent). Extract <math>\tau</math> and its 1σ uncertainty.
| step5_label = Compute Q
| step5_text  = <math>Q_\psi = \omega_0\tau/2 = \pi f_0\tau</math>. At 40 kHz: <math>Q_\psi = \pi \times 40000 \times \tau</math>. Pass criterion: <math>Q_\psi > 200</math> corresponds to <math>\tau > 1.6</math> ms.
| step6_label = Cross-check with VNA bandwidth
| step6_text  = Measure S11 resonance on the same cavity using VNA. Extract <math>Q_L = \omega_0/\Delta\omega_{3\text{dB}}</math> from the −3 dB bandwidth. Agreement between ringdown Q and VNA Q to within 15% confirms measurement validity.
}}

== ψ-Dependent Extensions ==

=== Soliton-Enhanced Effective Q ===

The <math>-\lambda\psi^3</math> nonlinear term supports soliton solutions that partially resist the decay that would occur in a purely linear resonator. The effective Q of a soliton mode in the nonlinear regime is higher than the linear Q = 1/tan δ_ψ — the soliton uses energy stored in the nonlinear field to compensate material loss, at the cost of a slight frequency shift from the linear resonant frequency.

Quantitatively, for a soliton of amplitude <math>\psi_s</math> in a medium with linear loss <math>\Gamma = \omega/Q_{\rm mat}</math>:

:<math>Q_{\rm eff} = Q_{\rm mat}\left(1 + \frac{3\lambda\psi_s^2}{m_{\rm eff}^2 + 3\lambda\psi_s^2}\right)</math>

For <math>\psi_s^2 \gg m_{\rm eff}^2/(3\lambda)</math>: <math>Q_{\rm eff} \to 2Q_{\rm mat}</math> — the soliton effectively doubles the quality factor. This is a direct mathematical consequence of the master equation's nonlinear structure.

Track B: this predicts that the measured ringdown time during a high-amplitude Anchor burst (soliton regime) is longer than during a low-amplitude test pulse (linear regime). Comparing ringdown times at two drive amplitudes provides a direct test of the soliton-enhanced Q mechanism.

=== Q Measurement as ψ Field Verification ===

The Q_ψ measurement protocol described above is Track A — it measures the electromagnetic properties of the composite resonator. However, if the [[ψ Falsifiability Framework]] (F1) predicts that the ψ field should produce specific anomalous effects in the ringdown (e.g., anomalously slow decay during operator presence, beyond what the EM Q alone predicts), then deviations from the electromagnetic ringdown model become direct experimental evidence for ψ.

Specifically: if a trained operator maintains sustained intentional focus during a ringdown measurement, and the measured <math>\tau</math> is significantly longer than the EM-predicted <math>\tau = 2Q_{\rm mat}/\omega</math>, this excess ringdown time is attributable to ψ field contributions to the stored energy <math>U_\psi</math> — contributions not present in the empty-cavity measurement. This is the Mode Geometry Test ([[Mode Geometry Test]] F3) in its Q-measurement formulation.

